# Engine event content repair

Scope: issues 112 and 113 on exact engine parent
`ec720020fd5dd4a23f097df2956152d46cdcda7a`. The older Bunny fixer branch is
preserved separately; this branch does not merge its release range.

## Admission and required behavior

Update callbacks must retain this save's original content through integration,
and integration must receive its saved current content. Named type-change
callbacks must use the current engine's assigned/original receiver contract.
Absent event options must be omitted; present invalid values remain rejected.

Runtime ownership is separate: the runtime agent confirmed that 8700e40ad
changes imports and atomicSave only. This repair changes only the update
afterCommit integrate event snapshot, not transaction/unknown-outcome handling
or upload cleanup.

## Reproduction on the engine source

The independent attacker corrected the absent changing-key fixture in
`5550233293bab858d37bd5f718085865e08da5b2`. Its copied test file remained
byte-identical, SHA-256
`615c8b1d52c415ef88fd5dd9ea024028a13f1810dafb8f02e089283eb24e9169`.

Before the production repair, its thirteen tests reported twelve passed and
one failed. Validate, store and finalize observed `before`; integrate observed
undefined instead. All three issue113 callback-selection cases passed.
Therefore no new issue113 production correction is justified on this source.
The older Bunny source's callback selection findings are not reattributed here.

The integrate eventCard reconstructed the dirty fields and type/receiver
identity but omitted both content values. The repair supplies current content
from the store snapshot and original content from the save's original row.
The empty string and null remain distinct values.

## Checks and limits

After the correction, the unchanged attacker file passes all thirteen cases.
The additional update-integrate-content suite checks original string, empty
string and null against actual saved current content/type using own synthetic
SQLite copies. Existing update and save-path suites are included in the same
run: four Vitest suites, thirty-six passed, zero failed/skipped.

The Node source suites for type changes, dirty conditions and event override
precedence report sixty-three passed, zero failed/cancelled/skipped/todo. These
exercise actual source and local SQLite; no model, remote backend or original
site was invoked. Existing transformation caches may be reused, not verdicts.

The first copied attacker file failed TypeScript checking on four fixture
declarations: three observedModEventRuntime calls omit required fetcher, and
the viewer declares unsupported bot. The attacker was notified to correct
the fixture without changing assertions. No registry guard was weakened.

The attacker supplied typed fixture commit
`7ece0f9881b8b81fca52037950f9a4830ba41c47`. Inspection confirms that its diff
adds the required fetcher contexts and a supported signed-in bot viewer;
assertions are unchanged. Both copied/attacker files have SHA-256
`0212ef6f99f1068f9cc36238d9bca19968547a751b065c8c04b7a3896868269c`.
The final typed fixture and four-suite run again pass thirty-six tests,
with zero failures/skips. Whole-tree TypeScript checking passes.

The exact typed fixture was also rerun with a Vite source-load override that
supplies only the action engine's exact ec720020f blob at its original module
path. It again reports twelve passes and the one integrate-prior-content
failure; all three issue113 cases pass. The production working file was not
reverted. The baseline source, override, full failing output and JUnit are
retained locally under `.runtime/events112/`; `after.xml` retains the complete
four-suite passing output. The override is an isolated verification instrument,
not a second application or a deployed target.

This is an engine regression correction, not a full-port, hosted-backend or
deployment acceptance result. Independent re-attack on the controller's exact
integrated candidate remains required.
